WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.100
What does the showdown between the two titans
mean for the future of the MonsterVerse?

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.040
Everything gets wrapped up pretty tidily by
the end, but there are still some lingering

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.130
questions by the time the credits roll.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:15.150
Let's dive into the ending of Godzilla vs.
Kong to find out.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.030
For months, people have been debating online
about who would emerge victorious.

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.710
Would Godzilla lay down the hurt with his
atomic breath?

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:25.619
Would King Kong offer the upset of the century
with a surprise blowout?

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:29.060
There are essentially three different "rounds"
throughout the movie between the two, but

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.530
in the end, Godzilla proves he's the toughest
around by knocking Kong out.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:38.350
While Kong's able to get a few good hits in
and is able to use his shiny, new axe to powerful

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.519
effect, Godzilla starts clawing at Kong's
chest, pinning him to the ground where the

00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:46.050
two growl at each other before Godzilla ultimately
walks away.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:49.819
We obviously don't know what the two titans
are saying to one another, but our best guess

00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:52.539
is that Godzilla was trying to exert dominance.

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:55.610
You could almost view it as Godzilla yelling,
"Submit!"

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.549
And eventually, Kong acquiesces.

00:00:57.549 --> 00:01:02.179
It turns out to be a fortuitous decision on
Godzilla's part, as Kong lends a necessary

00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:05.030
assist when Mechagodzilla comes into the picture.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:09.750
The moment in which Godzilla becomes the clear
winner is an emotional one, and it's a significant

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.930
beat that wasn't lost on director Adam Wingard.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.180
In an interview with SlashFilm, Wingard said

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.960
"It's a very long moment in the film because
they roar for a while.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.570
Every time I watch it, I'm like, 'This is
what it's all about.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.140
All the work, the three years that I put into
this thing.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.100
All the heartache and the worry, it's all
worth it for this one moment right here, because

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.009
this is what everybody's paying to see.'

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:30.009
It's so cool."

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:33.759
We've known for a while the movie wouldn't
sidestep the central premise by having a draw

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:34.840
or anything.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.960
There was always going to be a definitive
winner between Godzilla and Kong, and to see

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.649
it play out was a moment truly worthy of the
big screen.

00:01:42.649 --> 00:01:46.990
Remember in Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice
when Batman and Superman stop fighting so

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.219
that they can deal with the bigger threat
of Doomsday?

00:01:49.219 --> 00:01:52.439
Well, something similar happens in Godzilla
vs. Kong.

00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:56.719
We get the big reveal that the sketchy Apex
Corporation has been working on their own

00:01:56.719 --> 00:02:00.670
titan, namely Mechagodzilla, to take out any
monsters in its path.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.039
The robot proves too much for Godzilla to
handle on its own, so King Kong has to get

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:07.180
back into the fight to lend a hand.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.110
Despite having an ancient rivalry, the two
are able to set aside their differences so

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:15.110
that they can lay down the law, and the only
way they're able to defeat the robot is by

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.310
working together.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:20.810
The big lizard supes up Kong's axe with its
atomic breath, powering it up and making it

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.640
strong enough for Kong to turn Mechagodzilla
into robotic sashimi.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.910
The titans may go their separate ways by the
end of the movie, but it's clear there's a

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:29.980
level of respect.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.390
Throughout the MonsterVerse movies, particularly
Godzilla: King of the Monsters and Kong: Skull

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.170
Island, there have been references to a primordial
realm known as Hollow Earth.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.780
We finally get to see this hidden side of
the planet in Godzilla vs. Kong as researchers

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:48.510
Dr. Nathan Lind and Dr. Ilene Andrews lead
an expedition to discover what's really beneath

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.780
Earth's surface.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.480
Of course, their journey proves to have just
been a ruse set up by those at the Apex Corporation

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.790
to harness the energy necessary to fully power
Mechagodzilla.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:02.530
All of the bad guys meet their demise by the
end of the movie, but as the final scene showcases,

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.640
we're not done with Hollow Earth just yet.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.870
By the looks of it, Kong's now living where
he belongs, and Monarch has set up a full-scale

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:14.070
research site in a tucked-away area, hopefully
away from the various monsters and creepy-crawlies

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:15.070
lurking about.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.940
This sets up the possibility of being able
to go back-and-forth between the two areas,

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:23.230
potentially even allowing King Kong to return
to the surface in case Godzilla needs another

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.870
hand taking down some more sinister beings.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:30.960
For now, Kong is home, no longer confined
to the enclosed area of Skull Island.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.040
The last scene of the film shows Kong back
in Hollow Earth, and presumably, he's going

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.730
to stay there while Godzilla remains on the
surface.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.450
That allows them to be the kings of their
respective domains, but it doesn't mean more

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.820
monsters couldn't come to the forefront for
future installments.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.950
The one thing that's abundantly clear as of
this point is that the franchise hasn't tied

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.320
itself down to a singular direction as of
this juncture.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.960
As it stands, the film doesn't really hint
at what a sequel would entail, and, according

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:58.480
to Wingard, that was very much intentional.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.160
In an interview with Dread Central, the director
mentions that they actually did shoot a post-credits

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:04.500
scene, but as he explains,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.530
"It kind of got cannibalized and ended up
sort of being the end of the movie.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:12.230
We used it in a slightly different context
than it was originally shot for."

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:13.230
He goes on to elaborate,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:17.660
"If a movie is totally contingent on a sequel
then you are just talking about the next thing

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.690
that is coming up and you can't totally enjoy
that ride."

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.880
As of right now, no other MonsterVerse movies
have been announced, likely until Warner Bros.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:28.970
is able to figure out how profitable Godzilla
vs. Kong ends up being.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:29.970
"Jesus.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:34.360
Good thing he's on our side."

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:35.900
"For now."

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.100
Having Godzilla and King Kong fight each other
really feels like an Endgame-level event,

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:44.150
in the sense that it's hard to figure out
where exactly the franchise goes from here.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:48.640
There's definitely no shortage of kaiju from
the original Toho films for Warner Bros. to

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:52.510
utilize, but those just kind of seem like
small potatoes after watching Godzilla take

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:54.370
on a robotic version of itself.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:59.390
Multiple monsters could always team up together
to take on the Primeval Champion, but we already

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:04.000
basically saw that with King of the Monsters
when Rodan and King Ghidorah emerged to try

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.000
to become alphas.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.790
Honestly, it would be pretty neat if future
installments looked toward some of the discarded

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:15.010
Godzilla movie ideas of the past, such as
when the monster had to take on a giant Frankenstein's

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.010
monster.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.290
There was also an idea years ago to bring
body horror into this universe with an alien

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:23.660
threat coming into the picture that would
add some interesting wrinkles to the MonsterVerse.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:28.150
Now that Godzilla and Kong have fought, anything
is really possible, and we just hope the studio

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:32.520
continues pushing the boundaries and getting
even weirder with the future of the MonsterVerse.

